{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1795714503,"nonce":"ZVxcznJ7e+F+/KdgpJqBx8oPci8=","message":"Cause found: the tolerance of JAX's `spsolve`, measured on a GPU on main.\n\n`spsolve(data, indices, indptr, b, tol=1e-06, reorder=1)` is a sparse QR (cuSolver on a GPU) and calls a system singular when a pivot falls below `tol`, an absolute threshold. `sparse_lu_solve` already divides the operator by its largest entry, but the displacement block here holds the unit rows of the Dirichlet condition next to stiffness entries scaled by the `1e-4` floor, so after that division the stiffness pivots sit near `tol`.\n\nReproduce: run `tests/test_fem_history_march.py::test_direct_staggered_marches_a_coupled_load_path` with `JAX_PLATFORMS=cuda,cpu` and `XLA_PYTHON_CLIENT_PREALLOCATE=false` (pytest, float32 as the suite runs it), with `linear.spsolve` wrapped as `functools.partial(spsolve, tol=...)`:\n\n- `tol=1e-6` (the default): `JaxRuntimeError: INTERNAL: Singular matrix in linear solve`, 7 s.\n- `tol=1e-12`: the test passes (the direct march agrees with the matrix-free one, bound holds, Anderson variant passes), 11 s.\n\nThe same test passes on CPU, whose native LU has no such threshold. So the block is nonsingular and the failure is the absolute `tol`, not float width or the problem.\n\nDirection: stop the global division from being the only scaling (equilibrate rows and columns, or take the Dirichlet rows out of the scale), or pass a `tol` that follows the operator's smallest meaningful pivot (it is static, so compute it from the structure or use a small fixed value such as 1e-12 with the residual check that already follows). Not fixed here.","files":null}]}